  <section>
    <h2>The question</h2>
    <p>
      MetaCLIP-2 and SigLIP-2 cover hundreds of languages in one dual encoder, but
      image–text retrieval still shows a 30<sup>+</sup> pp Recall@1 gap between
      high-resource languages (HRL-6: en, fr, de, es, zh, ko) and low-resource ones
      (LRL-5: bn, fil, hi, sw, te), reproduced on XM3600, Flickr30k-200, XTD-200 and
      Babel-ImageNet. Benchmark-level analyses report that the gap exists; they do not
      say <em>where along the encoder's forward path</em> the representations diverge.
      We localise it by intervention.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>1. The linear language direction is a symptom, not the cause</h2>
    <p>
      Language identity is trivially linearly extractable from the output text
      embedding: a 13-way logistic-regression probe reaches <strong>99.8%</strong> on
      MetaCLIP-2 and <strong>99.7%</strong> on SigLIP-2 (chance 7.7%). Averaging the six
      HRL embeddings of a parallel caption also lifts retrieval by
      <strong>+17.4 pp</strong>. The natural hypothesis is that this direction crowds out
      alignment-relevant geometry.
    </p>
    <p>
      It does not. LEACE erases the direction to chance and retrieval barely moves — and
      moves exactly as much as a same-rank <em>random</em> orthogonal ablation. Iterated
      INLP up to rank 128 attenuates the classifier to 37–50% with the same null result.
    </p>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table class="results">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>XM3600 i2t R@1 (%)</th>
            <th>MC2 HRL</th>
            <th>MC2 LRL</th>
            <th>SL2 HRL</th>
            <th>SL2 LRL</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td class="method">Base embedding</td>
            <td>78.1</td><td>44.1</td><td>63.3</td><td>21.7</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="method">LEACE (classifier → chance)</td>
            <td>77.5</td><td>43.0</td><td>64.0</td><td>21.6</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="method">Random rank-12 ablation</td>
            <td>78.2</td><td>43.6</td><td>63.0</td><td>21.8</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="table-caption">
      LEACE drops the language classifier from ~99.8% to 3–5%, yet no cell moves more
      than 1.5 pp and LEACE is indistinguishable from a random projection of the same
      rank. A linearly visible feature is not, by itself, evidence of a causal one.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>2. The causal factor is the EOS forward-path trajectory</h2>
    <p>
      We probe the forward path with single-row activation patching. At layer ℓ, the
      target caption's EOS hidden state is overwritten with its parallel English value,
      every other token row is kept, and the frozen back-half runs unchanged. Retrieval
      is then scored against the fixed image gallery.
    </p>
    <figure>
      <img
        src="/geometric-bottleneck/img/patch_sweep.png"
        alt="EOS patch layer sweep on MetaCLIP-2, HRL and LRL tier means against the English reference"
        loading="lazy"
      />
      <figcaption>
        Tier-averaged XM3600 i2t R@1 across EOS-patch layers on frozen MetaCLIP-2 (1,000
        images). The rescue grows toward the near-final region rather than peaking at a
        confined layer.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
    <p>
      We headline ℓ = <em>N</em>−4, which still passes through three unmodified back-half
      blocks. LRL retrieval rises from 44.1% to <strong>73.4%</strong> (+29.3 pp) on
      MetaCLIP-2 and from 21.7% to <strong>66.6%</strong> on SigLIP-2 — on a
      causal-attention and a bidirectional-attention encoder alike. Three controls
      separate this from a pooled-position tautology:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Mid-token patch.</strong> Patching a non-EOS row at ℓ = <em>N</em>−1
        leaves LRL retrieval essentially unchanged — the effect is specific to the
        pooled row.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Random-English EOS.</strong> An English EOS from an <em>unrelated</em>
        caption collapses retrieval below baseline (44.1 → 7.7 on MetaCLIP-2). The
        rescue needs parallel <em>content</em>, not an English-distributed vector.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Norm-matched Gaussian.</strong> Destroys retrieval entirely (HRL ~80 →
        8.5%, LRL → 1.6%), so the effect is signal-specific rather than norm-driven.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The rescue is also <strong>not English-specific</strong>: a parallel French EOS
      lifts Swahili to 78.7%, and the mean of the six HRL EOS states lifts it to
      <strong>88.0%</strong> — above the English single-source reference. That is what a
      trajectory account predicts: a multi-HRL centroid sits closer to the interior of
      the parallel-content cone than any single EOS.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>3. Trunk calibration: the same diagnosis, at training time</h2>
    <p>
      Patching needs an oracle parallel caption at inference. As a general-input test of
      the same diagnosis, we soft fine-tune only the <strong>first <em>M</em>
      text-encoder blocks</strong> (<em>M</em>=4 for MetaCLIP-2, <em>M</em>=3 for
      SigLIP-2) so each language's projected output moves toward the parallel-content
      anchor, while the back-half, projection head and vision tower stay frozen.
      Training uses text-only parallel-translation groups over a 1M-caption CC12M
      subset; ~50M / ~40M parameters are trainable.
    </p>
    <figure>
      <img
        src="/geometric-bottleneck/img/tier_summary.png"
        alt="Tier-mean retrieval on XM3600: frozen, trunk-calibrated, and EOS-patched"
        loading="lazy"
      />
      <figcaption>
        XM3600 (1,000-image subset) tier means. The trunk recovers a substantial fraction
        of the LRL gap from general input; the oracle patch, which is handed parallel
        content at inference, goes further.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

