  <section>
    <h2>4. What calibration does to the geometry</h2>
    <p>
      Per-layer cross-lingual CKA on 1,000 image-aligned XM3600 captions shows the
      predicted reshaping. In the frozen encoder, the HRL–LRL curve sits about
      0.10–0.12 below the within-HRL curve near the projector input — the geometric
      counterpart of the retrieval tier gap. After calibration, HRL–LRL CKA rises from
      0.458 to 0.556 at ℓ=23 on MetaCLIP-2 and from 0.302 to 0.451 at ℓ=26 on SigLIP-2.
    </p>
    <figure>
      <img
        src="/geometric-bottleneck/img/cka_layers.png"
        alt="Per-layer cross-lingual CKA on MetaCLIP-2 and SigLIP-2, frozen versus trunk-calibrated"
        loading="lazy"
      />
      <figcaption>
        Solid: frozen. Dashed: trunk-calibrated. The shaded band marks the trunk-trained
        front blocks (ℓ &lt; <em>M</em>). Only the front blocks are trained, yet the lift
        appears at the final block — the frozen back-half stays competent on the shifted
        region.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
    <p>
      The trajectory divergence itself falls at every depth, with the tier gap shrinking
      from 0.119 to 0.022 on MetaCLIP-2 and from 0.143 to 0.045 on SigLIP-2. Across the
      ten non-English trained languages, that same frozen divergence correlates
      negatively with a WebLI-100B pretraining prevalence proxy (Spearman −0.88 /
      −0.64) — corroborating context, not a second claim, at <em>n</em>=10.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>5. It is not specific to the MetaCLIP-2 / SigLIP-2 pair</h2>
    <p>
      We replicate the frozen gap, pooler-row patching, and the same trunk recipe on
      three architecturally distinct encoders that differ in pooling regime, text-tower
      scale, and pretraining objective: <strong>AltCLIP</strong> (XLM-R-Large,
      CLS-pooled, 24L), <strong>NLLB-CLIP-L</strong> (NLLB-200 encoder,
      language-code-pooled, 24L), and <strong>mSigLIP</strong> (SigLIP-base, sticky-EOS,
      12L).
    </p>
