  <section>
    <h2>Takeaway</h2>
    <p>
      Linearly extractable language identity at the output and the alignment-causal
      mechanism are different phenomena. Concept erasure removes the identity direction
      with little change to retrieval; the causal factor is the per-language
      forward-path trajectory of the pooled hidden state. For multilingual dual-encoder
      design, this argues for intervening on the <em>trajectory</em> rather than the
      output embedding — per-language drift is already expressed at the depth-<em>M</em>
      EOS hidden state, which makes the front blocks a natural correction site while the
      back-half stays frozen.
    </p>
    <p>
      These measurements also form a retraining-free diagnostic checklist for new
      multilingual vision-language encoder releases: per-layer cross-lingual CKA, LEACE
      on the output embedding, EOS-patch sweeps at ℓ ∈ &#123;<em>N</em>−4, <em>N</em>−1&#125; with
      parallel-EN / random-EN / mid-token controls, and per-language back-half
      sensitivity.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Scope.</strong> What we establish is an interventional <em>localisation</em>
      within trained encoders, not a complete causal account of why the divergence
      arises during pretraining. Tokenisation, attention regime, optimisation dynamics
      and data prevalence all remain live upstream candidates. Trunk calibration also
      narrows but does not close the tier gap, and it requires parallel-text supervision
      that passes an MT-quality screen.
    </p>
  </section>

